  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA dangerous and prolonged heat wave will impact the region through \nFriday. An anomalously strong and broad H500 high pressure \ncenter will continue to build into the region, moving directly \noverhead on Wednesday. Afternoon temperatures will climb into \nthe mid to upper 90s across the lowlands under light SW flow and\nmostly to partly sunny skies. These temperatures and dew points\nin the lower 70s will create an oppressive humidity and heat \nindex values in the mid 100s. Some locations may see heat \nindices nearing 110 degrees Wednesday and Thursday afternoons. \n\nDid lower dew points and temps away from the NBM due to known \nwarm bias with these types of events, especially for Thursday \nwith some drier air anticipated to mix down in the afternoon. \nEven with doing this heat index values across most of the area \nrange between 95 and 110 degrees each afternoon. Heat safety \nshould be exercised this week.\n\nThe Extreme Heat Warning has been expanded eastward to cover the\ncounties of Tyler, Doddridge, Clay, Braxton, Gilmer, Lewis, and\nHarrison as confidence has increased in higher heat index values\nand impacts across these counties. The Heat Advisory still\nstands across our mountain counties Dickenson, VA up through\nBarbour and Taylor in WV. The higher elevations of Raleigh,\nFayette, Nicholas, Webster, Pocahontas, and Randolph are\ncurrently outside of dangerous heat criteria. Will continue to\nmonitor trends with forecasts updates to determine the need for\nfurther product expansion. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nH500 high pressure center is currently over the Cumberland\nPlateau region of TN and KY. Broad axis of ridging is stationed\nover our area with some notable perturbations in the isobars.\nWhile most locations will remain dry with partly cloudy skies,\nthe chances do exist for isolated afternoon showers or\nthunderstorms this afternoon and possibly again on Thursday. \n\nSubsidence increases tonight and Wednesday as the high pressure\ncenter migrates over our southern coalfields and mountains. As \nsuch, Wednesday will most likely be the driest day, after\nfleeting chances for morning showers pass.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nHigh pressure center shifts east on Friday causing the ridge to\nbreak down. During this time, the pattern will gradually become\nactive with diurnal showers and thunderstorms as a tropical \nmoisture and shortwave disturbances move through the area.\n\nModels are still divergent at this point, especially due to the\nmessiness of this upcoming pattern, but scattered to numerous\ncoverage is increasingly possible by Sunday. The main driver\nwith this will be a front approaching from the north which will\nset up shop across our northern forecast area early next week.",
